  "answer": "The learned structural features already capture the relevant patterns in the data, so the representation does much of the work that a complex model would otherwise have to do. Because the features are informative and make classes more separable, even simple, low-capacity learners (such as linear models, naive Bayes, or shallow decision trees) can reach good accuracy, with less tuning, less data, and lower computational cost. The features can be used with any classifier, but the benefit is largest with simple algorithms that cannot learn complex structure from raw inputs on their own.",
